      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm reviewing our HACCP plan for our catering operations. Can you confirm the specific temperature and time requirements for the cooling stage of cooked poultry? We need to ensure we are strictly mitigating the risk of Clostridium perfringens growth.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, when it comes to cooling cooked poultry, it's crucial to keep the temperature down to prevent the growth of Clostridium perfringens, a notorious foodborne bacteria. The temperature should be reduced from 135°F (57°C) to 70°F (21°C) within two hours, and then to 41°F (5°C) or lower within an additional four hours. This rapid cooling is essential to keep the bacteria at bay, as they thrive in the warm, moist environment of cooked poultry. Remember, these are the guidelines set forth by the Food and Safety Modernization Act, and adhering to them is key to ensuring the safety of your food. Keep an eye on your cooling process, and don't let any time slip by without checking the temperature. Your food safety is paramount, and these steps will help you maintain it.",
